<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The whole app bundle, minus the binary. There is no Xcode project, no
  storyboard and no asset catalog: `simctl install` accepts a directory with
  this file and an executable in it, and winit's `run_app` supplies the
  `UIApplicationMain` that Info.plist's `CFBundleExecutable` names.

  Three keys here are load-bearing rather than boilerplate:

  * `UILaunchScreen` (an EMPTY dict is enough, iOS 13+). Without a launch
    screen -- storyboard or this key -- UIKit runs the app in a 320x480
    compatibility box letterboxed inside the real screen, and the map draws
    into a postage stamp with black bars. That is not an error anywhere; it
    just silently looks wrong.
  * `CFBundleSupportedPlatforms = [iPhoneSimulator]`, which is how `simctl
    install` tells a simulator bundle from a device one.
  * `CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone`. Absent it, iOS imposes a MINIMUM
    frame duration of 1/60 s on every iPhone app, and a CAMetalLayer hands
    out drawables no faster than that however quickly the app asks.

    It is NECESSARY and it is NOT SUFFICIENT, which is the part worth
    writing down. This key lifts a ceiling; it does not raise the rate. A
    process that has never told Core Animation what cadence it wants is
    given the iPhone default of 60 whether the key is present or not, and
    measuring it says so plainly -- `ios/src/app.rs`'s `[perf]` line, same
    build, same phone, same idle map:

        no key,  no display link    59.2 fps   p50 16.66 ms
        key,     no display link    61.7 fps   p50 16.65 ms   <- unchanged
        key, plus a display link    78.0 fps   p50  8.33 ms touch-to-present

    The thing that actually asks is `ios/src/display_link.rs`; that file has
    the reasoning. This key is what makes the asking legal.

    Note that even together the two are a ceiling lift and not a floor:
    Core Animation still picks the rate, and the `[perf]` histogram shows it
    moving between 60 and 120 as the app's own load changes. Nothing here
    forces 120 Hz on a scene that cannot sustain it, which is correct --
    a map that is not moving has no business holding a panel at 120.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
    <string>en</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>gpui-map-demo</string>
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>dev.gpuimaps.iosdemo</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>gpui-map</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>gpui-map</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>0.1</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>1</string>
    <key>CFBundleSupportedPlatforms</key>
    <array>
        <string>iPhoneSimulator</string>
    </array>
    <key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
    <true/>
    <key>MinimumOSVersion</key>
    <string>13.0</string>
    <key>UIDeviceFamily</key>
    <array>
        <integer>1</integer>
    </array>
    <key>UILaunchScreen</key>
    <dict/>
    <key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
    <array>
        <string>arm64</string>
        <string>metal</string>
    </array>
    <key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
    <array>
        <string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
    </array>
    <key>UIStatusBarHidden</key>
    <true/>
    <key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
